the sanitiser guards foreign text, not the head's own — and takes a sequence whole

The operator's screen, live for the first time on the 07:04 binary, from my own
`81990b3`: **`/notes` drew its rows as garbage** — literal `[31m` and `[0m` as printable
text, and the wrapping broken so words fell onto their own lines. `81990b3` said it guarded
"the slash listing — the echo and every row of `/notes`, `/job`, `/gate`". It did, and
that was the mistake: **a `/notes` row is this head's own composed text, already coloured
by the head.**

Reproduced on the bytes first, coloured, through the real producer:

     [31m! gate — a refusal [0m

The `ESC` gone and the body left behind. Two faults in one place.

## Fault one: §3.1's subject is content the head did NOT write

A note this head painted with its own colour is not foreign content. Pointing the
sanitiser at it strips the head's own escapes — the same bug with the sign flipped.

**Which of the six sites I was wrong about**, since that list is the interesting half:

| # | the site closed in `81990b3` | foreign? | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | a tool-progress note (`card` header tail) | yes — the daemon's `ToolProgress` | **right**, kept |
| 2 | the queued echo (`queued_lines`) | yes — the operator's words, and a *paste* can carry a control byte | **right**, kept |
| 3 | **the slash listing** | **no — this head's own rows** | **WRONG** |
| 4 | the four panes (job command/state, subagent prompt/role/state, todo content, picker title, header title) | yes — the model's and the daemon's words | **right**, kept |
| 5 | the five raw `sgr::REVERSE` sites | not a sanitiser question | **right but unrelated** — they emitted escapes under `color: false`, which is `Palette::None`'s contract broken; now wrapped in `colour()` |
| 6 | **`Palette::paint` sanitising its argument** | **no — a palette's argument is the head's own text, and it may already carry the head's own sequences, because this method is called on strings the head composed *by calling this method*** | **WRONG** |
| — | **`card::render`'s body** (found by re-reading, not by a test) | **no — `body` is composed by the caller** | **WRONG** |

Three wrong, and the third is the shape of all of them: **a guard placed where the string
happens to pass rather than where foreign text enters.**

## Fault two, and it is the more general defect: a sequence must go whole

`without_control` replaced every control character with a space. For an escape sequence's
**introducer** that is the worst of both: the `ESC` becomes a space, the `[31m` stays, and
the result is **five columns of visible garbage where the terminal measured none** — which
is also why the wrapping broke, because the wrapper counts what is on the line.

So it now consumes the sequence: `ESC [ params final`, `ESC ] … BEL`/`ST`, and the C1
forms (`CSI`, `OSC`, `ST`) with no `ESC` in front. A well-formed sequence measures no
columns, so **removing it leaves the column count exactly as it was** — a space would add
one. A **lone** control byte that is not part of a sequence (a tab, a `\r`, a DEL) still
becomes a space, because dropping it would silently reflow the line.

## A third instance of the same defect, found by the sweep

`sessionlog`'s `truncate_target` — which builds a tool call's display target out of the
model's **own arguments** — had the identical bug, and it is why the raw-markup row still
read `{"path":" ]0;pwned "}` after the head was fixed: **the `ESC` was already destroyed
upstream, so no later guard could see a sequence at all.**

That decides where the one definition lives. `letibot-sessionlog` cannot see
`letibot-ui` and must not start, so `without_control`/`without_control_lines` and their
sequence parser move to **`letibot-transcript::sanitize`** — the crate both sides already
depend on, for the same reason `ToolEditExcerpt` lives there. `letibot_ui::text` becomes a
forward, so every existing call site in this tree is unchanged.

**That adds one dependency edge, and `letibot-ui`'s manifest states a rule against
letibot crates.** The rule's reason is about *types* — "a rendering primitive that knows
what a `ToolOutcome` is cannot be tested without the transcript" — and a pair of pure
`&str -> String` functions is not that. The manifest now says so, in place, with the
alternative named: a second copy of a sequence parser, which is the duplicate this tree
keeps deleting.

## Tests, and two of them found holes rather than confirming

- `a_slash_listing_does_not_strip_the_colour_the_head_composed_into_it` — **the
  reproduction**, both halves: no escape's body as text, *and* the head's own colour still
  there (stripping it would be a different bug from leaving garbage).
- `text::an_escape_sequence_goes_whole_and_never_leaves_its_body_behind` — every family in
  the store's vocabulary, asserting **not one byte of any sequence survives** and the
  surrounded text does.
- `text::removing_a_sequence_leaves_the_bytes_and_the_text_behind_it` — the arithmetic:
  removal, not substitution.
- `text::a_lone_control_byte_becomes_a_space_rather_than_nothing`, and the pair that
  keeps the two functions apart.
- **The §3.1 sweep found two more things**, which is what it is for: `truncate_target`
  above, and that the sweep's own slash-listing case had been feeding raw foreign text
  straight into a surface whose contract is *already-composed rows* — a test of a contract
  no producer has. It now goes through `notes_lines()`, the real producer.
- Two of my own new tests were wrong on first run and both were my error, not the code's:
  a hand-typed expected string for the width property (replaced by the property itself),
  and a bare `CSI` case that correctly swallows the next byte as its final.

```
cargo test -p letibot-tools       613 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-tui         317 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored
cargo test -p letibot-harnessd --lib 114 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-ui          103 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-sessionlog   96 passed, 0 failed
cargo test -p letibot-tokencore    61 passed, 0 failed
```

**Not verified:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run (it writes a temp file, which the gate
blocks). And the fix is verified on the bytes rather than on a terminal — the operator's
screen is the next thing that can confirm it, because this head is one of the two drawing
it.

## What I should have done in `81990b3`

Asked of each site *"is this foreign content, or my own?"* before closing it, rather than
after. The sweep test that found six holes was the right instinct and it was pointed at
the wrong question: it asked *"does an escape reach the terminal"* when the question is
*"does an escape THIS HEAD DID NOT WRITE reach the terminal"*. A test that cannot tell
those apart cannot tell the fix from the bug — which is the same family as the night's
other finding, that a liveness check which cannot be false is not a check.
